<BaseLayout title="Waynesboro Infrastructure Agreement | Who Is Abebe?">

  <Header>
    <Fragment slot="title">Waynesboro Infrastructure Agreement</Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="subtitle">
      The June 15, 2026 agreement between the City of Waynesboro and
      Abebe Ventures, LLC, as it appears in the city's council packet.
    </Fragment>
  </Header>

  <main class="article-wrap">
    <article class="article-body report-page">

      <p class="lede">
        On June 15, 2026 the Waynesboro council received an agreement with
        Abebe Ventures, LLC for the grant-funded infrastructure at St. George
        Crossing. This page lists what the city's own documents say about it,
        what a party says, and what is still open.
      </p>

      <div class="notice-box">
        <strong>Scope:</strong>
        Everything below about the agreement comes from the six-page copy in
        the city's June 15, 2026 council packet. That copy is unsigned. The
        signed agreement and the state award documents were not found online.
        Budgets and agreements are not proof that money was paid.
      </div>

      <h2>Update, October 2026: What the Record Says</h2>

      <h3>Parties</h3>

      <p>
        The agreement is between "CITY OF WAYNESBORO ... and ABEBE VENTURES, LLC,
        a Georgia limited liability company, hereafter called 'the Developer,'
        whose address is 249 Milton Ave SE Atlanta, GA 30315". The notice
        address reads "Attn: Mike Abebe".
      </p>

      <h3>Who holds the grant</h3>

      <p>
        The packet names OneGeorgia Rural Workforce Housing Initiative Award
        25wh-MCR-5-5229. The Governor's release of December 5, 2024 says "The
        City of Waynesboro was awarded $2,496,190 in OneGeorgia funds". It names
        the City as recipient and does not name the developer. The state
        program's own questions-and-answers document says "Local
        government/City/County must be the primary applicant." The agreement
        says the City designs and builds the infrastructure, draws down the
        funds, owns and maintains it, and reports to the State.
      </p>

      <h3>The three amounts</h3>

      <table class="report-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Source of funds</th>
            <th>Amount</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>OneGeorgia grant, awarded to the City</td>
            <td>$2,496,190.00</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>City contribution</td>
            <td>$100,000.00</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Developer costs</td>
            <td>$423,810.00</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Total cost, as the agreement states it</td>
            <td>$3,020,000.00</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        The agreement also says: "The Developer will be responsible for any
        additional costs that exceed the grant award." The council's draft
        minutes say the developer "will be responsible for all project costs
        exceeding the grant award and City contribution."
      </p>

      <h3>What must be built, and at what prices</h3>

      <p>
        Section 1.1 requires "the design, development and construction of
        twenty-four (24) townhomes and twenty-seven (27) single-family
        residences, and the Developer shall not sell or allow the sale or
        purchase of any single residence for less than $125,000.00 or more
        than $290,000.00." Section 1.4 says the residences are to be sold "to
        individuals who will use any of the residences as a primary residence".
      </p>

      <h3>Default and repayment</h3>

      <p>
        Section 1.5 lists what counts as default, including that the Developer
        has "failed to complete construction of all 51 residences", "stopped or
        abandoned construction of any residence for a period of more than 90
        consecutive days", or sold a residence for more than $290,000.00 or for
        less than $125,000.00. It also lists bankruptcy and receivership.
      </p>

      <p>
        On default, the agreement says the City "may immediately terminate this
        Agreement" and the Developer "shall immediately remit or repay any
        amount of the Award, and any other assessment or penalty to the State of
        Georgia might impose." It adds that this "includes the Developer
        indemnifying the City of Waynesboro for any Award funds that the State
        of Georgia demands be returned due to a default by the Developer."
      </p>

      <p>
        Sections 1.6 and 1.7 say the Developer "shall convey to the City, at no
        cost, fee simple title to any and all permanent or temporary easements
        and rights of way" and "shall provide proof of funding to the City and
        DCA ... at the time of execution of this Agreement." Section 2.1 sets the
        term "until the Developer has sold the last residence within the
        Project".
      </p>

      <h3>The vote</h3>

      <p>
        The draft minutes of June 15, 2026, printed in the July 20, 2026
        packet, say: "Council Member Byne made a motion to approve ... Vice-Mayor
        Bates seconded the motion. Council voted unanimously in favor of the
        motion". The minutes record no discussion of the item. Final approved
        minutes were not read.
      </p>

      <h3>The state program's rules</h3>

      <p>
        The state's award procedures manual says that if a home "is not sold in
        the specified price range, the infrastructure grant is subject to
        repayment to the OneGeorgia Authority." In that manual the "recipient"
        is the local government.
      </p>

      <h2>Update, October 2026: What a Party Says</h2>

      <p>
        The Governor's release describes the award as money "to construct
        street, drainage, water, and sewer infrastructure that will allow for
        the construction of 51 new, housing units in St. George Crossing. The
        development is on a 190-acre tract." That is the state's description of
        the project.
      </p>

      <p>
        True Citizen reported on June 16, 2026: "Abebe Ventures will be
        responsible for any additional costs that exceed the grant award."
      </p>

      <h2>Update, October 2026: Open Questions</h2>

      <div class="fact-grid">
        <div class="fact-card">
          <span>Open Question</span>
          <p>
            The packet copy is unsigned. The developer's signature block reads
            "ABEBE VENTURES, LLC / By: ____" with no name or title. Who signed,
            and when, is not shown.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="fact-card">
          <span>Open Question</span>
          <p>
            "EXHIBIT A DESCRIPTION OF PROPERTY" is a heading with nothing under
            it. The agreement says the property is "set forth in EXHIBIT A", so
            which parcels it covers is not shown in this copy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="fact-card">
          <span>Open Question</span>
          <p>
            These six pages show no date or number of months by which the 51
            residences must be finished, and no bond, letter of credit, escrow
            or other security from the developer. The agreement binds the
            developer to the terms of "the Award". The award documents were not
            found, so they may contain terms this copy does not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="fact-card">
          <span>Open Question</span>
          <p>
            The state's questions-and-answers document says both "thirty (30)
            months from award date" and "the two-year timeframe". Which period
            applies to this award is set by its award documents, which were not
            found.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="fact-card">
          <span>Open Question</span>
          <p>
            On September 17, 2026 the Utilities Committee draft minutes record
            five bids and call a base bid of $2,930,454.00 from Shockley
            Plumbing, Inc. the "apparent low bidder". The September 21, 2026
            agenda lists "Resolution 2026-09-01 to Award". The minutes of that
            meeting are not posted, so the award vote and the contract amount
            are not confirmed. The agreement's three amounts total
            $3,020,000.00. The pages read do not say how engineering and
            administration costs fit within that total. The contract's owner is
            the City. No Abebe entity is a party to it in the pages read.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

